            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Overall, your house has a fair bit of excess air leakage, however there are some key areas where air leakage occurs. 



I noticed air leakage through electrical outlets on your exterior walls. I recommend that you outfit any electrical outlets located in exterior walls of your house with electrical wall plate gaskets.  



I recommend replacing the weather stripping along all of your exterior doors, particularly if it appears damaged or worn. Using a screw in aluminum style weather stripping may help to create a better seal, as well as using an adjustable strike plate to ensure the door makes good contact with the weather stripping while closed.  



You may also notice air leakage at your baseboards and at corners between walls. 



While the fan in your upstairs bathroom appeared to work well while I was there, I recommend you replace your exhaust fans with an ENERGY STAR?????? rated model, which will have a new damper built into the new unit. I also recommend that you install a bathroom fan in the main floor bathroom, as it's currently boarded up. 



The attic access hatch had some air leakage entering from the attic space. I recommend using a removable caulking to seal up the access hatch. 



Savings: For a minimum 10% improvement you could potentially save $130 per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by 3.9 GJ.



To be eligible for an air sealing rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 studs with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) of extruded polystyrene insulation to all exterior walls of the home.



Savings:

By adding R15 of extruded polystyrene insulation, you could potentially save $810 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 23.7 GJ, and qualify for up to$2500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program

Under the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, the rebate you receive for insulation will be double that of the AEA?????????s rebate program for energy-efficient products (the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program).



To be eligible for an insulation rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>

The attic of the second floor of your house was assumed to have R32 of fiberglass insulation. I have modelled adding  blown cellulose insulation for a total insulation value of R50. As the savings for this upgrade are negligible, I do not recommend making this upgrade a priority.



The second floor of your house represents approximately 52% of the total ceiling area of your house, and the remaining ceiling area is the cathedral ceiling in your living room. The costs involved in upgrading the insulation in cathedral ceilings is often prohibitive, and this is not recommended as an upgrade.  



Savings:

By increasing the insulation to R50, you could potentially save $14 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 0.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $65 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total ceiling area and achieve a minimum insulation value of RSI 7.0 (R-40). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of ceiling area that is insulated compared to the total ceiling area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program

Under the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, the rebate you receive for ceiling insulation will be double that of the AEA?????????s rebate program for energy-efficient products (the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program).



To be eligible for a ceiling insulation rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</ceilings>
                <foundation>

Your basement walls appear to be 8 inches of concrete with no insulation. I have modeled adding 3 inches (R15) rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation.



Your basement pony walls appear to be 2x6 framing with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation. I have modelled adding 3 inches (R15) rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to the pony walls. 



I have not modelled any upgrades to your crawlspace, as the floor above the crawlspace appears to be well insulated. This space could have the heat duct removed or sealed off, and additional insulation could be added. Please contact me to discuss this. 



Savings:

By adding 3 inches (R15) rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to the basement walls, as well as 3 inches (R15) rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to the pony walls, you could potentially save $1,065 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 31.0 GJ, and qualify for up to $437.50 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total foundation wall area including crawlspaces (when applicable), and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.8 (R-10). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program

Under the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, the rebate you receive for basement insulation will be double that of the AEA?????????s rebate program for energy-efficient products (the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program).



To be eligible for a basement insulation rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</foundation>
                <windows>

Some of your windows are older double pane ones that do not appear to have a Low E coating or an insulating gas fill between the panes of glass.These windows are in reasonable shape, but I did notice some physical damage around some of them. I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgradingall of your older windows with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



If you cannot replace all of your windows at once, I recommend starting with the windows in your bedroom, as you mentioned that this room is particularly cold. 



Savings: 

By replacing all of your older windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially save $370 per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by 10.8 GJ.  Windows that are ENERGY STAR?????? qualified and rated for climate zone 3 are eligible for $250 per window in rebates, to a max of 12 windows through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, but are not otherwise eligible for a rebate. 



To be eligible for a windows rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



</windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 182 litre electric hot water tank. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly. I recommend that you replace your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



Savings:

By replacing your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, you could potentially save $1,080 per year in energy costs, increase your annual energy usage by 0.9 GJ, and qualify for up to $1,250 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program 



</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current oil fired furnace was originally rated at 80% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE. 



Savings:

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower, with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially save $2,290 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 53.6 GJ, and qualify for up to $1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program

Under the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, the rebate you receive for the furnace will be double that of the AEA?????????s rebate program for energy-efficient products (the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program).



To be eligible for a furnace rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>

ADDITIONAL COMMENTS



DRYER VENTING

Your dryer is currently vented inside to the basement. While we discussed this and you said you do not use your dryer frequently, Arctic Energy Alliance does recommend that all non-condensing dryers be vented to the outside of the house. 

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



ECM PUMPS

Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for water circulation pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power while operating, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the water and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. These pumps determine the minimal output required to keep the water lines from freezing. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ?????????learn????????? the system?????????s usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick, and Arctic Energy Alliance if now recommending them for the circulation pump on your city water supply line. As this pump is for potable water, a stainless steel pump is required. When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house, you can qualify for $175 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



HEATING OPTIONS

While I modelled an upgrade to propane for your furnace, you were also unsure whether you wanted to change fuels. I have also modelled an upgrade to a more efficient oil furnace so you could compare. 



Your current oil fired furnace was originally rated at 80% AFUE. I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency oil furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, which also incorporates an ECM fan blower motor. If you choose a high-efficiency oil furnace (direct vented out a side wall), the current chimney can be eliminated altogether.



Savings:

By replacing your heating system with a sealed combustion oil-fired furnace with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, that also incorporates an ECM fan blower, you could potentially save $1,040 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 30.8 GJ, and qualify for up to $1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program





Another alternative is to replace the furnace with a boiler and fan coil unit. This would allow the existing ductwork to be used. If you would like more information on this option, please let me know and we can discuss further. 





---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



FUEL COSTS

The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $17.81 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.29 per litre 

Propane - $0.80 per litre

Wood - $341.25 per cord 

Pellets - $5.51 per bag

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



DISCLAIMER

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



YOUR ENERGY ADVISOR

Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Alexandra Giroux

Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: alexandra.giroux@aea.nt.ca



Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!

</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
